          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>The 24-year-old farmer presents with bradycardia, pinpoint pupils, excessive sal</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>The 24-year-old farmer presents with bradycardia, pinpoint pupils, excessive salivation, and wheezing. Which toxidrome does this clinical picture represent?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Anticholinergic toxidrome</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The anticholinergic toxidrome presents with the opposite picture: tachycardia, dilated pupils, dry skin, and hyperthermia. The farmer's wet, constricted-pupil presentation indicates cholinergic excess.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cholinergic toxidrome</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Bradycardia, miosis, excessive secretions (salivation, sweating), and bronchospasm are hallmarks of cholinergic excess from acetylcholine accumulation at muscarinic receptors, requiring atropine and pralidoxime.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Sympathomimetic toxidrome</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The sympathomimetic toxidrome causes tachycardia and mydriasis with diaphoresis. The farmer's bradycardia and pinpoint pupils point to cholinergic excess, not sympathomimetic stimulation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Opioid toxidrome</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While opioids also cause miosis and reduced consciousness, they characteristically produce respiratory depression without the bronchospasm, profuse salivation, or bradycardia seen here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The farmer's bradycardia, pinpoint pupils, salivation, and bronchospasm represent cholinergic excess. This pattern — the opposite of the dry, tachycardic woman — demands immediate atropine and pralidoxime."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The combination of bradycardia, miosis, and excessive secretions defines the cholinergic toxidrome — recognising this pattern within seconds guides life-saving treatment with atropine and pralidoxime."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
