        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 309.575,
            "to": 319.575
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Both the sympathomimetic and the anticholinergic toxidrome cause tachycardia and</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Both the sympathomimetic and the anticholinergic toxidrome cause tachycardia and mydriasis. What single clinical feature best distinguishes between them?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Presence of delirium</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Delirium can occur in both toxidromes — anticholinergic delirium and sympathomimetic agitation overlap considerably, making this an unreliable discriminator.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Elevated blood pressure</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hypertension may be seen in both toxidromes. Sympathomimetics commonly raise blood pressure, but anticholinergic tachycardia can also elevate it. Sweating is the more reliable sign.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Presence of diaphoresis (sweating)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Sympathomimetic toxidrome produces sweating because sympathetic activation drives sweat glands, whereas anticholinergic toxidrome leaves the patient bone dry due to muscarinic blockade of sweat glands.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Degree of pupil dilation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Both toxidromes produce mydriasis (dilated pupils), so pupil size alone cannot differentiate them. The presence or absence of sweating is the defining distinction.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key discriminator is sweating. Sympathomimetic toxidrome produces tachycardia with sweating, while anticholinergic toxidrome produces tachycardia with completely dry skin. Both cause mydriasis and can cause delirium."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Excellent. Diaphoresis is the key discriminator — sympathomimetic patients are wet, anticholinergic patients are dry. This single sign guides entirely different management strategies."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
