          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 30-year-old woman presents after drinking toilet-cleaning acid 45 minutes ago.</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 30-year-old woman presents after drinking toilet-cleaning acid 45 minutes ago. She has severe oropharyngeal burns and can swallow with difficulty. Which action is appropriate?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Administer activated charcoal via nasogastric tube</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Activated charcoal is contraindicated in corrosive ingestion — it does not adsorb corrosives and can obscure endoscopic assessment while causing additional mucosal injury.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Neutralise the acid with an alkali solution</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Neutralisation is contraindicated. The exothermic reaction generates heat that deepens the chemical burn, causing more tissue damage than the original corrosive.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Perform gastric lavage to remove residual acid</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Gastric lavage risks oesophageal perforation and re-exposes the mucosa to the corrosive agent. It is strictly contraindicated in corrosive ingestion.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Allow small sips of water or milk to dilute the corrosive</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. In a conscious patient who can swallow, small volumes of water or milk dilute the corrosive. Endoscopy should follow between 6 and 24 hours to grade the injury and guide stricture surveillance.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "In corrosive ingestion, activated charcoal, gastric lavage, and neutralisation are all contraindicated. The correct action is small sips of water or milk for a conscious patient who can swallow, followed by endoscopy at 6–24 hours."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. For corrosive ingestion in a conscious patient, dilution with small sips of water or milk is appropriate. Never lavage, give charcoal, pass a blind nasogastric tube, or attempt neutralisation."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
