          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient recovering from organophosphate poisoning develops sudden respiratory </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient recovering from organophosphate poisoning develops sudden respiratory failure on day 3. Which delayed complication is most likely?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Organophosphate-induced delayed polyneuropathy</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>OPIDN occurs 2–4 weeks after exposure and presents with progressive limb weakness, not acute respiratory failure in the first few days.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Aspiration pneumonia from the acute phase</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While aspiration can occur during the initial cholinergic crisis, new-onset respiratory failure at day 3 in a recovering patient is characteristic of the intermediate syndrome, not a delayed aspiration event.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Intermediate syndrome</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The intermediate syndrome occurs 24–96 hours after organophosphate poisoning and causes sudden respiratory failure from persistent neuromuscular junction blockade, even as muscarinic signs are resolving.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Recurrent cholinergic crisis from atropine withdrawal</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>If atropine has been adequately dosed and the patient was improving, recurrence of the cholinergic crisis is unlikely at day 3. The timing and presentation point to the intermediate syndrome — a nicotinic phenomenon at the neuromuscular junction.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The intermediate syndrome occurs 24–96 hours after organophosphate poisoning, causing sudden respiratory failure from persistent neuromuscular junction blockade even as muscarinic signs resolve. OPIDN, by contrast, appears weeks later with limb weakness."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The intermediate syndrome causes respiratory failure 24–96 hours post-exposure due to persistent neuromuscular junction blockade. Anticipating this in a seemingly recovering patient is critical for survival."
                }
              ],
